          <tr><th scope="row">Video</th><td>fal.ai Hailuo 2.3 Standard, Kling 2.5 Turbo Pro</td><td>Live</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Music</th><td>fal.ai Stable Audio</td><td>Live</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Narration</th><td>ElevenLabs voice model hosted by fal.ai</td><td>Live via fal</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Narration (direct)</th><td>ElevenLabs official API</td><td>Not qualified: no account key held</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Assembly</th><td>fal.ai ffmpeg compose</td><td>Live</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Masked edit</th><td>No qualified vendor edit route yet</td><td>Not built</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Direct ElevenLabs access is the one provider gap: the studio uses ElevenLabs' model through fal's hosted endpoint, billed by fal, and says so. ACE Music is excluded from this delivery entirely — no adapter, no key, no promise.</p>
    <h3>What it cost</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Request-level spend for this run. Rows marked <code>est.</code> are planning estimates; none of these figures is invoice-confirmed, and the vendor invoice remains authoritative.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Object</th><th scope="col">Route</th><th scope="col">Cost</th><th scope="col">Outcome</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {costs.map(([name, route, cost, result]) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{name}</th><td><code>{route}</code></td><td>{cost}</td><td>{result}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
        <tfoot><tr><th scope="row">This run</th><td>sound, assembly and the portrait cut</td><td>US$0.2035</td><td>US$0.1435 sound + US$0.06 estimated renders</td></tr></tfoot>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Hosting is separate and shared: the studio runs on the same Workers Paid account as the rest of the estate, with R2 storage for originals and exports. Idle runtime does not bill per request, but the shared paid account and the stored bytes cost money whether or not anyone is rendering — there is no zero-cost idle state. Recorded request-level usage across both studio projects is US$1.50, of which US$1.22 is the Paper &amp; Light project shown here. That is not a monthly price: it is what these specific requests cost.</p>
    <h3>What is not claimed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>No true 9:16 export. The portrait derivative is 4:5 and it sorts portrait takes ahead of the rest, which changes the order you selected. Both are open defects, not settings.</li>
      <li>No masked inpainting, outpainting, background removal or upscaling. Those need a qualified vendor edit route and a real mask tool; until then the interface does not offer them.</li>
      <li>No voice cloning, no training on anyone's likeness, no rights workflow for either.</li>
      <li>No member roles on the independent studio. It is one owner session; the wider Composer grant model is a separate, unfinished piece.</li>
      <li>No full non-linear editor, no 4K, no 30-second single inference, no guarantee of identity across takes.</li>
      <li>The films are screen recordings with cuts and time compression stated in the captions; they are not one uninterrupted take.</li>
      <li>Caption and audio checks were made with loudness and duration analysis and by reading the transcripts; a by-ear review and a physical-device matrix were not performed.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="repeat" class="prose">
    <h2>Repeat it</h2>
    <p>You need a Cloudflare account on Workers Paid, a supported agent, and — for video, sound and rendering — a funded fal.ai key. Creating those accounts, signing in and approving spend are yours; the agent does the technical work. You do not need Docker, Wrangler or a database seed to start.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="/init?start=media-studio">Build your studio</a>: choose Media Studio, name it, pick defaults, save the draft.</li>
      <li>Export the specification and copy the agent handoff. Check the pinned source and the human-only actions.</li>
      <li>Let your agent create your copy, apply the migrations, add the secrets and deploy. It should read back the live revision.</li>
      <li>Open the studio from the Composer menu. Upload something, make a still, generate a bed, build a storyboard, render it, publish one piece.</li>
      <li>Come back with a change you actually want. That is the part that makes it yours.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The <a href="/media-studio/tutorial">illustrated tutorial</a> carries the same recipe chapter by chapter, with what you do, what the agent does, what you should see, and how to recover. The <a href="/media-studio/media-studio-brief-20261114.json" download>portable brief used here</a> is a real exported example, not a sketch.</p>
    <p class="small">Source: <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/composer-media-studio">loftwah/composer-media-studio</a> (MIT) · <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer">loftwah/app-composer</a>. Oyster, Prints and Darkroom are Levi's MIT-licensed Fungi projects. The studio keeps their attribution and licence notices.</p>
